ISSUE:
JsonReader silently accepts \uXXXX escapes containing lone surrogates in string values and field names

**The problem**

`JsonReader.readEscapeCharacter()` ([`JsonReader.java:1855`](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1855)) reads 4 hex digits, casts to `(char)`, and returns unchecked at [line 1882](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1882). No `Character.isSurrogate` / `0xD800..0xDFFF` gate anywhere. Callers at [line 1244](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1244) (string-value) and [line 1340](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1340) (skip-string) consume the raw char with no validation. Both `setLenient(false)` and `Strictness.STRICT` accept the malformed input.

**The specs**

- [RFC 8259 §8.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259.html#section-8.2): "behavior of software that receives JSON texts containing such values is unpredictable; for example, implementations might return different values for the length of a string value or even suffer fatal runtime exceptions."
- [RFC 7493 §2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7493.html#section-2.1) (I-JSON): "Object member names, and string values in arrays and object members, MUST NOT include code points that identify Surrogates or Noncharacters as defined by [UNICODE]... `\uDEAD` is invalid because it is an unpaired surrogate."
- [RFC 8785 §3.2.2.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785.html#section-3.2.2.2) (JCS): "occurrences of such data MUST cause a compliant JCS implementation to terminate with an appropriate error."

**Reproducer (gson 2.14.0, JDK 21, `Strictness.STRICT`)**

```java
import com.google.gson.*;
import com.google.gson.stream.*;
import java.io.StringReader;

public class Repro3113 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String[] docs = {"{\"k\":\"\\uD800\"}", "{\"k\":\"\\uDC00\"}", "{\"\\uDC00\":1}"};
        for (String doc : docs) {
            JsonReader r = new JsonReader(new StringReader(doc));
            r.setStrictness(Strictness.STRICT);
            r.beginObject();
            String name = r.nextName();
            String val = r.peek() == JsonToken.NUMBER
                    ? String.valueOf(r.nextInt()) : r.nextString();
            System.out.println("input=" + doc
                + " ACCEPT name.char0=0x" + Integer.toHexString(name.charAt(0))
                + " val.char0=0x" + Integer.toHexString(val.charAt(0)));
        }
    }
}
```

Output:
```
input={"k":"\uD800"} ACCEPT name.char0=0x6b val.char0=0xd800
input={"k":"\uDC00"} ACCEPT name.char0=0x6b val.char0=0xdc00
input={"\uDC00":1}  ACCEPT name.char0=0xdc00 val.char0=0x31
```

**How to read the output**

- `input=` shows the JSON document fed in.
- `ACCEPT` means the parser returned successfully (no exception).
- `name.char0` is the hex value of the first char of the field name.
- `val.char0` is the hex value of the first char of the value.
- Row 1: field name is `"k"` (`0x6b`), value's first char is `0xd800` — a lone high surrogate returned as-is in the Java `String`.
- Row 2: same field name, value's first char is `0xdc00` — a lone low surrogate returned as-is.
- Row 3: field NAME itself starts with `0xdc00` (a lone low surrogate); the value is just the digit `1` (`0x31`).

All three should have rejected under `Strictness.STRICT` per the RFC 7493 / RFC 8785 requirements above.

**Suggested fix**

The return at [`JsonReader.java:1882`](https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/stream/JsonReader.java#L1882) is the site to add the guard. Rejecting under `Strictness.STRICT` preserves current behavior in `Strictness.LEGACY_STRICT` / `Strictness.LENIENT` — no Android-compat break.

